  56: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
  57:     """
  58:     Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.
  59: 
  60:     Input structure
  61:     - The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.
  62:     - The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.
  63:     - Exactly one object has the largest area.
  64: 
  65:     Rule
  66:     - Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.
  67:     - Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.
  68:     - Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.
  69: 
  70:     Key insight
  71:     - The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.
  72:     """
